WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.820
Hi, welcome back to the Lam Rim (LAM RIM):
Love in the Times of the Virus.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.870
We are on episode four.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:23.760
I called episode four "The Best and the Worst." You can see an image here of a couple, and they look really happy.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:34.400
They are unreal. They're too good-looking. They're both too good-looking. It is like Seiji and his friend.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.060
You know this story and I know this story.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:57.500
People ask me, "Geshe-la, I want to give up desire. I want to live a simple life. Should I give up having a boyfriend or a girlfriend, or a wife or a husband?

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.660
Should I give that up?"

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:10.660
I have to say--from many, many years of experience--that having a good partner, especially a spiritual partner, 

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:17.260
is the happiest thing you can do in your life, if it's going well.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:25.640
On those perfect days, it really is like this photograph and it does feel like magic.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:35.380
I know people who had a difficult relationship. Then they gave up relationships for ten years, twenty years, thirty years.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:47.460
I don't know... it seems very sad to me. I think you can make it work if you understand the seeds, and it can be the most beautiful thing in your life.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:58.300
I think it's the most beautiful thing in your life. 
But you know and I know that when it goes bad, 
the most beautiful thing in the world changes

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.000
to the most terrible thing in the world.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:11.320
And there's nothing like that suffering of staying 
with somebody in the same house or the same 
apartment and you used to love each other,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.300
and now you really hate this person more than anybody, or they just bother you all the time,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.640
and you feel tension all the time.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.780
So this episode, number four,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:34.600
we call it the best and the worst because
there seems to be that when something can 
be very beautiful, it can also be very terrible.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:41.080
It just depends on how well you can keep the seeds going, what kind of seeds you can plant.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:48.740
But it does seem to be, in life, that the most beautiful 
things can turn into the most terrible things.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.040
Now why are we talking about that? 
Let's go back to the text.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.770
ma sung na ni (MA BSRUNGS NA NI)--

00:02:55.770 --> 00:03:06.640
Pabongka Rinpoche was describing 
how cool it is if one person is nice to other people,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:15.880
especially in hard times, especially in difficult places 
and difficult times in history, like right now.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:25.760
People who are sweet to each other, 
people who share things, people who treat each 
other with respect and love in these difficult times,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.180
then they are... it's like a Buddha 
came back to this planet.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:45.680
Those people are special. And I have to say, to do small nice things for other people during these dangerous times, is the sweetest... I mean, it's going to make you happy.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.060
It's like a direct highway to happiness.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:58.820
Of course it's a terrible thing, 
of course it's hurting everyone, 
but this is the time when you can be a real Buddhist.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.040
You can be a Buddha. You can 
be a really special person now.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:05.050
So these are good opportunities.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.480
Now, what if we give in to that selfishness?

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:17.740
What if we have these emotions? 
I don't know about you, for me it's when I can't sleep.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:24.800
It seems like more people are having trouble 
sleeping nowadays. Of course! It's a dangerous time.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:31.600
It's a time for a lot of worries. 
We're worried about ourselves, 
we're worried about other people.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.540
So, what if we give in to that instinct and we say, "Okay. 

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.380
Oh, there's ten packs of toilet paper. 
I'll buy all of them. Forget the other people." Or,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.380
"My money's tight right now, so 
let me call the church or the temple or the mosque, and

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.620
let me cancel that donation I make every month.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.940
I'll tell them my... 'this is difficult. I'm having trouble right now. I can't make that donation.'

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.000
Forget the Gold Club.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.470
I'll cut the donation to the Gold Club."

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.060
So, it's tempting to think that way. 
Now, what the text says:

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.320
ma sung na ni, gelong la rabtu chepay do
(DGE SLONG LA RAB TU GCES PA'I MDO)

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.040
There's a sutra called "I Love Being Nice."

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.390
There's a sutra called "I Love Being Nice"-- 
rabtu chepa (RAB TU GCES PA).

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:26.660
Seiji has it in his text: dak chendzin (BDAG GCES 'DZIN), 
which is "self-cherishing."

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:33.520
Here we have chepa (GCES PA), meaning
I really "love," and I "value" being good to other people.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.852
That sutra says: la lay tsultrim dewa te, la lay tsultrim dukngel yin,
(,LA LA'I TSUL KHRIMS BDE BA STE, ,LA LA'I TSUL KHRIMS SDUG BSNGAL YIN,)

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.380
tsultrim denpa dewa te, tsultrim chelwa dukngel lo
(,TSUL KHRIMS LDAN PA BDE BA STE, ,TSUL KHRIMS 'CHAL BA SDUG BSNGAL LO,)

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:50.340
It's like the girlfriend or it's like the boyfriend.
la lay tsultrim dewa te--

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.520
some people's morality makes them happy all day.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.500
Some people's morality makes them suffer all day.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:09.180
tsultrim denpa dewa te--
when you keep your morality, when you are nice to other people, that's pretty much the definition of happiness.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.880
And, tsultrim chelwa dukngel lo--

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:21.840
when you break down, and in the days of the virus, 
you get selfish and you hold what you have,  
and you don't share and you're nervous

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.840
and you're tense and you treat other people badly,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:34.280
well, then breaking the same morality is the worst thing. 
So, morality--the vows, the commitments you 
have to be nice to other people--

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.899
they are like a boyfriend or girlfriend.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:45.320
They can make you incredibly happy 
or they can make you terribly sad and miserable.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:55.840
If you break those laws of morality,
if you treat other people... 
mean, and tense, and unhappy,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.760
then the same vows are going to make you miserable.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.620
Got it? Just like that girlfriend or that boyfriend.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:15.040
And I went and got it for you. I found it:
la lay tsultrim dewa te, la lay tsultrim dukngel gyu,
(,LA LA'I TSUL KHRIMS BDE BA STE, ,LA LA'I TSUL KHRIMS SDUG BSNGAL RGYU,)

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.960
tsultrim denpa deway chok, tsultrim chelpa dukngel yin,
(,TSUL KHRIMS LDAN PA BDE BA'I MCHOG ,TSUL KHRIMS 'CHAL PA SDUG BSNGAL YIN,)

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:22.260
shintu lekpar tsultrim gang, dampe mi de dzepay suk,
(,SHIN TU LEGS PAR TSUL KHRIMS GANG, ,BSDAMS PAS MI DE MDZES PA'I GZUGS,)

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.640
dulwa la ni nyewar ne, tsultrim lappa rang yi che,
(,'DUL BA LA NI NYE BAR GNAS, ,TSUL KHRIMS BSLAB PA RANG YID CHES,)

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:39.560
If you keep those good behaviors--if you treat 
other people nice--then it makes you 
beautiful and you can believe in yourself.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:51.060
If you share what you have in the time of the virus, 
for the rest of your life you can believe in you. You can believe in you.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:58.680
And if you break down now and you give in to selfishness, 
you will have to think about it for the rest of your life.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:11.680
I can tell you from having lived a long life, 
the times I was good to other people and 
I wasn't selfish, they gave me a lot of pleasure.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.780
I can think about them any time. I'm proud of them.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:20.920
rang yi che (RANG YID CHES) means then you can believe in yourself 
for the rest of your life.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.880
she dang, tsultrim yang dakpar denpay do le
(ZHES DANG, TSUL KHRIMS YANG DAG PAR LDAN PA'I MDO LAS)

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:37.260
Then there's another sutra that he quotes, which is the sutra about people who stay nice to other people.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:50.620
There's a very, very powerful line there, and I remember it. I've heard it spoken many, many times. And it's:

00:08:50.960 --> 00:09:00.080
gelong dak sok dang drel shing shiwa ni la-y,
tsultrim nyam shing shikpa ni de ta mayin no
(DGE SLONG DAG SROG DANG BRAL ZHING SHI BA NI BLA'I, TSUL KHRIMS NYAMS SHING ZHIG PA NI DE LTA MA YIN NO)

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:01.840
I'm going to say it again.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:10.020
gelong dak sok dang drel shing shiwa ni la-y,
tsultrim nyam shing shikpa ni de ta mayin no

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:18.800
gelong dak sok dang drel shing shiwa ni la-y,
tsultrim nyam shing shikpa ni de ta mayin no--

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:24.060
losing your life is a hard thing.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:33.500
Losing your life is a hard thing, 
but losing your morality is much, much worse.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:45.740
If you compare, breaking down 
during the time of the virus and 
becoming selfish is much worse than dying.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:50.180
It's better you died
than that you were selfish during the virus.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.700
That's weird. Right?
It's weird to say that.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:10:00.520
"Geshe-la, everybody was a little selfish. 
I just bought a little extra toilet paper. 
I'm not some kind of monster."

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:10.620
No! Buddha said a little bit of selfish behavior 
during the virus is worse than dying.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:16.240
And there's a debate about the spelling here. 
Those of you who know some Tibetan,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:25.820
la-y (BLA'I)--there's a word here, la-y, 
and if you spell it with B-L-A--ba'o latak la (BA'O LA BTAGS LA). 

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:33.780
If you spell it B-L-A, it means it would be "better" to die than to be selfish during the virus.

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:40.800
If you spell it with an S... 
and the spelling in the scriptures is about fifty-fifty.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:44.740
It'd be interesting to try to find 
the Sanskrit, because that would decide it.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.540
If you spell it with an S--sa latak la (SA LA BTAGS LA; SLA)--
it means much, much "easier."

00:10:50.380 --> 00:11:00.020
No problem. Dying is much, much easier
than being selfish during the time of the virus.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:08.020
Dying would be an easy thing compared 
to not sharing your money during the virus.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.940
"Whoa! What do you mean?"
And we have an image here.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:25.320
I've been spending a lot of time gardening because I'm locked down. I used to make Veronica do all the gardening and I used to enjoy the flowers.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.180
Now, she's like, "Okay, you're here, you've got to pull some weeds." So what you see here is...

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:43.840
You know gardening is not planting beautiful flowers. Gardening is sitting there on your knees and pulling weeds hour after hour. It's a dumb thing.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.840
In fact we had a debate.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:49.597
I said, "This is not a weed. I'm not pulling it out." 
She said, "It is a weed."

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.100
I said, "But it's a beautiful flower."

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:54.760
She says, "Yeah, it's a weed with a beautiful flower."

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:59.280
I said, "But, if it has a beautiful flower, 
it's not a weed. We should be growing it."

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.960
She said, "No, this is a weed." 
I said, "Who decided this is a weed?"

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:09.100
She said, "I don't know. Everybody decided this 
is a weed." I said, "But I like the flower! 
The bees also like the flower!"

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:12.189
We had a debate like that.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:21.200
Last week I bought some flowers for the fall 
because we have to plant different flowers 
for our bees. We have a bee hive.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:31.920
I found a flower... on the package it says in Hawaii this is a weed; in California, this is a garden flower.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.720
I showed it to her, "This is the emptiness of weeds."

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.100
Why am I talking about that?

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:51.120
When you pull a weed out, you have to be 
careful not to shake it, because when you 
shake it a hundred seeds fall out from the seed pod.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:58.360
When you pull a weed out, if you shake it, 
then it creates a hundred more weeds.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:12:59.403
See what I mean?

00:12:59.403 --> 00:13:01.620
Why am I talking about that?

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:08.060
Being selfish during the virus--
not sharing what you have during the virus--

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:16.000
it is like a weed and it's going to create a 
hundred more or a thousand more selfish events.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.320
You see that I mean?

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.540
It would have been better if you had 
died, just before you were selfish.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:26.920
Because if you're a little bit selfish with small things,

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:36.640
during difficult days when everyone needs 
you to not be selfish, then you're planting 
more seeds to be selfish in the future.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:38.040
Thousands of seeds.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.340
You are shaking a weed. It's a very interesting thing.

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:48.540
It would be better if you died than to be 
selfish in a small way during the virus

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:56.800
because that will create a thousand more 
selfish events and you will suffer much 
more than if you had died.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.380
Okay?

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:06.280
You know, it's not popular to say things 
like that in America. We're not supposed 
to say things like that, but it's true.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:17.820
It's true, and I didn't say it; the Buddha said it. Better to die than to be selfish in a small way during the virus.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:20.960
It doesn't mean you should go kill yourself.
It doesn't mean that.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:30.320
It means don't be selfish, be unselfish, and don't break your vows just because there's a virus going around.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:33.320
Your vows are supposed to last longer than that.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:43.720
You don't end here. This is not the end. If you 
catch the virus and you die--it's not the end. 
You will continue. You will live in another place.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:51.220
It's so funny. People say, "I don't believe in a future life."
I say, "Were you born?" "Well yeah."

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.600
"So, how were you born?"

00:14:54.280 --> 00:15:00.060
"Oh, causes and conditions, Geshe-la. Cause and effect. Small causes came together, I got born."

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:07.380
So I said, "Can it happen again?" They say, "No." 
I say, "In a thousand years could it happen again?"

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:15.120
"Well, maybe." "In a billion years can it happen again?" 
"I guess probably." "In a billion, billion years, 
could it happen again?" "For sure!"

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.200
"Okay, so you're going to be born again." Duh!

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:26.560
de chiy chir she na, sok dang drel shing shiwe ni kyewa di nyi kyi tse separ gyur
(DE CI'I PHYIR ZHE NA, SROG DANG BRAL ZHING 
SHI BAS NI SKYE BA 'DI NYID KYI TSE ZAD PAR 'GYUR)

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.860
Then Pabongka Rinpoche says why is the Buddha being so radical?

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.440
Why did the Buddha say it's better to die?

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:38.940
He said look, sok dang drel shing shiwe ni kyewa di nyi kyi tse separ gyur--

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:44.220
when you die, you just lose one life.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:49.740
When you die... in this world, during this virus, if you die,
you lose one life.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:55.000
tsultrim nyam shing shikpe ni kyewa bum trak gyay bardu rik dang drel shing dewa pang te--
(TSUL KHRIMS NYAMS SHING ZHIG PAS NI 
SKYE BA 'BUM PHRAG BRGYA'I BAR DU RIGS 
DANG BRAL ZHING BDE BA SPANGS TE)

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:04.380
but if you break down during the pressure of the virus, and you're selfish and you hurt other people--you don't share what you have with other people--

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:15.400
then you are affecting, he says, one-hundred times one hundred-thousand lives--ten million lives. You are affecting ten million lives.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:27.320
drel shing (BRAL ZHING)--you won't be happy, you won't 
be born into a good place with a with a good family,

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:37.720
and you are basically sacrificing happiness of ten 
million lifetimes by being selfish during the virus. You might as well die. It would be better if you had died.

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:41.540
pang te nampar tunwa chenpo nyam su nyongwar gyur ro--
(SPANGS TE RNAM PAR LTUNG BA CHEN PO NYAMS SU MYONG BAR 'GYUR RO)

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:47.000
you will have a terrible life for ten million lifetimes. 

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:54.040
"Oh, Geshe-la, it's too much. How can one 
small selfishness ruin ten million lifetimes?"

00:16:54.760 --> 00:17:02.340
"Oh, well, how can one cell from your mother, called 
an egg or an ovum, and one cell from your father,

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:08.680
in the sperm, how can they create your body?" 
I believe the number is one hundred-billion cells.

00:17:09.080 --> 00:17:13.140
You guys have to check for me. But the number 
of cells in the human body is about one hundred-billion. 

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:23.900
"Don't tell me two things can't create a hundred 
billion things, because the tongue you used to 
tell me that with is already proof that that's wrong."

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.580
It is kind of funny. 

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:41.100
I went and got this quotation from
the "Lam Rim Chenmo" (LAM RIM CHEN MO) by Tsongkapa.  
It says better to die. The word is "better."

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:52.140
Then I found it in the Kangyur (BKA’ ’GYUR), and it says "easier." 
It's the other spelling. It's an easier thing to die.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:55.040
So you guys can cook it. Maybe you guys can debate it.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:04.080
Then Pabongka Rinpoche quoted "Lung Nam Je" (LUNG RNAM 'BYED),

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:08.460
which is an old Vinaya sutra. I think it's misspelled here in the name.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.960
gangshik tunpa tukjey tenpa la, yanwar sem shing chung se da chepa,
(,GANG ZHIG STON PA THUGS RJE'I BSTAN PA LA,
,YANG BAR SEMS SHING CUNG ZAD 'DA' BYED PA,)

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:20.680
de ni de le dukngel shenwang tob, miktsel sekpe amay tsel nyam shin
(,DE NI DE LAS SDUG BSNGAL GZHAN DBANG 'THOB,
,SMIG TSAL BREGS PAS AA MRA'I TSAL NYAMS BZHIN,)

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:36.480
We're kind of going on to another subject and 
that's called... what do you want to call it? "dis-ing the rules." 

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:45.100
Like, "Okay, yeah, yeah, I'm supposed to share 
my toilet paper. Tell me something significant 
Geshe-la. You know, tell me about a vaccine, 

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:50.660
tell me about a trillion-dollar bailout, 
but don't talk to me about this little stuff. 

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:54.420
What, I'm supposed to share my toilet paper? 
Get real, Geshe-la.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.460
Talk about something big." 

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:12.000
So the next section of the lam rim is 
all about understanding that it's the small 
things--the Dharma is in the small things.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.880
The Dharma is in your small behaviors.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:23.720
It's not like inventing a vaccine or--that 
would be nice--or raising a billion dollars 
for the whole country to get a paycheck.

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:26.640
That's good stuff!

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:36.220
But, the amazing life is in the small things. 
The beauty of life is in the small things.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:41.900
It's a beautiful thing he says. So don't blow it off. 
That's the word I'm looking for. In American English,

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:52.600
we say don't blow it off. "Don't say all that. 
Come on, stop talking about toilet paper. Start talking about stimulus checks for ten billion dollars."

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:56.700
No, the success of your practice is in the small things.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:01.840
Don't blow off the small things. 
Many small things make a Buddha. 

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.300
de ni de le dukngel shenwang tob
(,DE NI DE LAS SDUG BSNGAL GZHAN DBANG 'THOB)

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:14.440
So a person who blows off the small things 
and says, "Oh, this is not so important. This thing is not that important." That person shenwang tob (GZHAN DBANG 'THOB).

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:22.120
This is a very famous expression in the Mind-Only School, shenwang (GZHAN DBANG). Do you guys remember? 
Who can tell me? For fifty dollars?

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:25.040
shenwang

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:27.500
Other...

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:32.060
Other power.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:34.520
Under the other power.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.519
Here you see it in a normal sentence.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:41.959
So shenwang is the 
Mind-Only School word for cause and effect really.

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:53.180
Because causes have to wait for... sorry, effects have to wait for causes. Causes are fat-cats, effects are their gofers.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:21:00.240
And effects have to wait on the fat-cats. The result 
has to wait for the cause. So anyway, shenwang.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:12.220
This person who blows off small details of being nice to other people, they say,

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:16.400
"Look, I'm a big Buddhist. I deal with millions 
of dollars. I don't hold doors open for people.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:20.600
I'm doing my Buddhism, Geshe-la, on a higher level."

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:23.520
Then you don't get it.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:26.440
The success of your practice is in the small things and 

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:29.640
when you blow them off, you are hurting the big things.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:33.140
And he gives an example.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:41.220
He gives an example, and it's kind of weird. 
You have to be in India to understand it.

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:52.940
The young mango trees, which are a big 
part of Indian life... If you live in India, the 
mango season is a joyous time.

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:56.420
It's a really, really happy time 
when the mangoes come in.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:06.540
The first mangoes are worth twenty dollars, and 
then a week later, they are worth five dollars. 
A week later they are worth fifty cents,

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:09.320
and a week later everyone's trying to give them away.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:17.900
So, anyway, mango trees are very valuable 
in India, and very delicious and very productive.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:23.020
But, they tend to grow weeds around them.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:33.660
Sometimes people will go out to cut 
the weeds with a sickle and they will 
cut the mango tree by accident.

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:38.820
So what's the example for, you guys? 
Thirty dollar question.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:43.440
He went to cut the weeds and he cut the mango tree.

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:50.840
He blew off...

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.160
He blew off...

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:58.950
He said, "Don't talk to me about  
sharing toilet paper. That's like weeds."

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:08.280
Yeah, but, if you ignore the weeds... if you don't 
treat the weeds right, you lose your mango grove.

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:10.380
Does it make sense? 

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:16.520
If you don't mow your lawn right, 
you give your car a flat tire. How's that?

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:19.700
Think about it.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:25.340
It means, finesse the small things in your 
Dharma practice, and then you protect the big things.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:29.670
But if you're sloppy with the small things, 
you're going to ruin the big things. How's that?

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:32.280
Famous example.

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:37.440
di na la le gyelpoy tsik chen yang, de na len ga chepa tob mingyur
(,'DI NA LA LAS RGYAL PO'I TSIG CHEN YANG,
,'DAS NA LAN 'GA' CHAD PA 'THOB MI 'GYUR,)

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:42.960
tubpay ka lung tsul min da che na, du gror drongyur a-lay dab lu shin
(,THUB PA'I BKA' LUNG TSUL MIN 'DA' BYAS NA,
,DUD 'GROR 'GRO 'GYUR AE LA'I 'DAB KLU BZHIN,)

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:47.430
So, in the...

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:56.900
I live in Arizona, and we are virus-stupid people. We don't like laws, we don't like to be told what to do,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:00.600
so we are like the worst state in America with the virus. 

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:06.720
It doesn't mean don't come visit me. 
We will get through it.

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:16.200
We have a huge state; it takes six or seven hours to drive to our retreat center, and you don't leave the state--

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:19.440
and we like to drive fast.

00:24:21.029 --> 00:24:26.088
This is the only state in America where you 
don't have to wear a helmet on a motorcycle.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:33.840
Because we like to go fast and we like danger. 
It's stupid during the virus. 

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:46.680
We get a lot of tickets. We get a lot of speeding 
tickets. The police love to sit there and catch 
Arizona people because we always go too fast.

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:57.760
Now, ever since I was a kid... when I was a 
little kid, my mom would go too fast. Four brothers in the car, and my mom is driving too fast,

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:00.340
and the police will stop her. 

00:25:01.769 --> 00:25:08.539
She never cried, ever. I never saw her cry. I mean sometimes, at a good movie or something.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:17.880
When the police stopped her, she starts crying, "Oh, oh! I have four kids in the car, I don't have enough food."

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:26.160
The police are, "Oh, it's okay. Next time don't drive so fast. Don't worry, I won't give you a penalty." It's really funny.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:37.240
And then she rolled up the window and 
the tears stopped. She said, "Okay kids, 
let's go. The cop, he gave us a break."

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:46.940
So here in the lam rim it says you can get a 
break from the authorities if you know how 
to talk, if you know how to cry,

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:53.820
sometimes you don't get punishment for the
things you do, but... what's he going to say?

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.040
In the world you can talk your way out of it but... 

00:26:04.319 --> 00:26:06.319
you cannot talk your way out of karma.

00:26:07.499 --> 00:26:10.968
You cannot talk your way out of karma. 
Karma is not like a policeman,

00:26:12.449 --> 00:26:15.769
who, if you start crying, the policeman's 
going to say, "Oh, it's okay. It's okay."

00:26:16.769 --> 00:26:18.329
Karma

00:26:18.329 --> 00:26:20.329
never has an exception.

00:26:20.459 --> 00:26:21.569
Karma

00:26:21.569 --> 00:26:23.040
always comes back to you.

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:26.140
tubpay ka lung tsul min da che na--

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:31.480
if you break even a small rule 
that the Buddha gave us,

00:26:31.619 --> 00:26:33.619
then

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:38.460
you're in for a bad time. Probably you will 
be born as an animal, he says here.

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:50.580
Then he says a-lay dab lu shin (AE LA'I 'DAB KLU BZHIN), and this 
is a weird thing in Sanskrit, called a-la. 
And a-la in Sanskrit is a cardamom seed,

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:55.060
that very delicious thing they put in Indian tea.

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:59.000
I have a picture here of an a-la plant.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:07.380
It's got very weird leaves. The leaves look very strange.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:11.560
I didn't number it, you guys; 
I just threw it in there. Sorry.

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:16.670
There you go. Yeah, that is a cardamom plant.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:22.099
It says here, you're going to end up like 
that cardamom plant guy.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:28.620
This is a reference to a person. And it's a beautiful story.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:31.880
So here's the story.

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:35.880
And I think it might have to do the virus, but

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:41.740
Buddhist people are supposed to 
respect the environment, okay. 

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:48.080
Buddhist people, we are required 
by Lord Buddha to respect the environment.

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:57.380
For example, monks and nuns are not 
allowed to cut a green plant without a special prayer.

00:27:57.680 --> 00:28:05.960
It's a vow—-we're not allowed to cut a 
green plant without a special ritual because,

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:11.820
Buddha said, you are destroying 
a city for tiny, tiny creatures.

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:15.740
It's like you bomb a whole city for tiny, tiny creatures.

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:21.860
There are tiny, tiny creatures that you 
cannot see who are living under the leaves,

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:26.899
around the plant, and when 
you cut the plant, or you kill a plant,

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:30.760
or you make pollution and you kill a whole Amazon,

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.600
you are killing billions of people's homes.

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:42.000
The word "person," "people," in Buddhism, 
doesn't distinguish between humans and animals.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:47.600
When you destroy the Amazon, you are 
destroying billions of people's homes.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:52.960
And you get the karma of 
destroying billions of people's homes.

00:28:53.820 --> 00:29:06.600
In the Vinaya, we're not even allowed to 
spit on a plant because we might hurt some 
of the small creatures who lived there. That's their city;

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:09.460
it might drown some of them. It's very interesting.

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:18.540
Anyway, there was a monk in ancient times, 
he read this tiny mini vow, mini monks' vow.

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:20.180
It's called a tung-che (LTUNG BYED).

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:29.100
It's a mini vow, and the mini vow is: 
don't spit on green plants and don't cut 
green plants without special purpose.

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:38.060
He was doing meditation in a cave, 
and he's go rek (MGO BREGS)--go rek means "shaved head."

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:47.560
There was an a-la plant 
growing on top of the cave, at the doorway.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:58.320
And every time he went out, it scratched his head... his bald head. Many times he said, "I've got to pull this thing out."

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:06.420
And then he remembered his vow. He said, "No, 
there are green... there are small creatures living there. Maybe there's a whole city of small creatures.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:11.880
I shouldn't pull it out." But, he kept scratching it.
One day he scratched it three times.

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:16.220
Then he said, "That's it. I'm pulling the
plant"... he pulled the plant out.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:20.449
That's bad, but then he said something worse.

00:30:20.449 --> 00:30:29.000
He said, "These stupid small rules the Buddha 
piles on us--two hundred fifty-three small rules for monks. It's too much!

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:31.220
What's he thinking? This is dumb!

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:35.130
These monk's vows are dumb!" 
He said something like that.

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:36.920
Guess what happened to him?

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:48.780
He was born as a naga, like a small dragon, 
and he had--I forget--seven heads or something. 
They all look like a cardamom plant.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:50.720
He looks like a walking cardamom plant.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:55.880
That's the story of this guy.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:31:01.660
I like the next picture. I'll show you the next picture.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:10.120
If you talk about how does Buddhism speak 
about the environment, protecting the environment, 

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:17.260
in the next picture here, you can see 
someone is drinking water from a stream. 

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:26.260
So, in the old days, two and a half thousand years ago, when the monks needed water, they just went to a stream and they drank.

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:39.592
But they're required by monks vows 
to check the water first to see if there are 
any small creatures on the top of the water.

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:47.300
We're even supposed to use a sieve. 
Monks are supposed to carry a sieve at all times.

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:52.740
They give you one nowadays, they give you a kitchen sieve--it's about this big--when you become a monk.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:58.600
It's funny because then you just keep 
it in your room for the rest of your life.

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:10.260
But in the old days, you were supposed to have 
a little cloth sieve that you keep in your belt all 
the time. And when you go to a stream to drink,

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:22.480
first you sieve the water, and make sure that 
nothing gets killed by you by accident. No tiny 
bugs get killed by you by accident.

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:28.220
And there's even an interesting rule in the monks vows that

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:39.580
if a group of monks goes down to the stream to drink, the guy to check with his sieve should be the nicest one.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:47.840
The rule is, if there's five monks, they should pick 
the most compassionate monk to check the water.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:33:00.720
So if a monk is not allowed to even take a sip 
of water from a small stream without 
protecting the creatures that live there,

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:09.820
then what's going to happen to a whole 
planet of people who destroyed the rivers 
of the whole world for billions of creatures?

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:14.000
What's going happen to those people?

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:20.220
That's the level that we're 
supposed to take care of our environment, okay?

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:27.900
I'm going keep going. I'm a little bit over, but... I'm sorry.

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:32.720
It's hard for me to guess how long it's 
going to take. I'm going to finish this one.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:36.720
jikten dir wang chewa gyelpo chenpoy ka lende na
('JIG RTEN 'DIR DBANG CHE BA
RGYAL PO CHEN PO'I BKA' LAS 'DAS NA,)

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:41.420
du gashik gi tse chepa jung la, ga shik la minjungway kab yu mu
(DUS 'GA' ZHIG GI TSE CHAD PA 'BYUNG LA, 'GA' ZHIG LA MI 'BYUNG BA'I SKABS YOD MOD,)

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:54.300
It's possible--if you're living in a country with a very 
cruel king--that sometimes they will kill you for little things and sometimes they'll let you go for big things.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:56.620
It just depends on the king's mood.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:02.280
tunpa tukje chen gyi che tsam le de na, gangshik gang gi tsende na-ang kun la chepa nyong te--
(STON PA THUGS RJE CAN GYI BCAS MTSAMS 
LAS 'DAS NA, GANG ZHIG GANG GI TSE 'DAS NA'ANG 
KUN LA CHAD PA MYONG STE,)

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:14.000
but if you break even a small rule set by the Buddha, 
for example, about destroying the homes of small creatures in streams or on leaves,

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:18.660
lu a-lay dapmay jungwa tar yin tsul--
(KLU AE LA'I 'DAB MA'I BYUNG BA LTAR YIN TSUL,)

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:22.480
the same thing will happen to you 
that happened to Mr. Cardamom Head.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.780
He's called Mr. Cardamom Head.

00:34:28.180 --> 00:34:32.980
Mr. Cardamom Head dragon. 
You will become like that.

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:38.740
lu a-lay dap kyang tung-che tsam kye du sepe--
(KLU AE LA'I 'DAB KYANG LTUNG BYED 
TZAM KHYAD DU BSAD PAS) 

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:43.780
in his previous life, that animal with the strange heads,

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:49.660
pulled the plant out that scratched his head and he said, "This is stupid rule." 

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:56.520
kye na... rang re pam hlak bumpo sok mangpo kye du supa di tar na, chimay drowa ji tar gyur--
(SKYES NA, RANG RES PHAM LHAG SBOM PO SOGS MANG PO KHYAD DU GSOD PA 'DI LTAR NA, PHYI MA'I 'GRO BA JI LTAR 'GYUR,)

00:34:56.660 --> 00:35:10.000
Then he says, "Look, Mr. Cardamom Head 
became an animal by pulling out a few leaves 
of a cardamom plant that scratched his head.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:22.800
You and me, we do much worse things 
many times a day. We are not talking you 
destroyed a few leaves of a plant.

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:32.540
We're talking you destroyed a planet of jungle and 
trees. You destroyed your whole country's ecosystem."

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:41.620
So he says ji tar gyur (JI LTAR 'GYUR)--"You think 
about it." It's funny, the lama's like, "you think about it!"

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:49.360
You decide what's going to happen to you. He 
stopped like that; he didn't go further with that.

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:54.200
All right, we finished that episode.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:59.080
It means be good to your planet.

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:12.820
Don't say, "This is a hard economy, 
we can use coal, we can burn coal for a while. We 
just had a virus and we need to get the economy going.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:18.940
It's okay to forget the environment for a while." 
And that's also really dumb.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:32.320
When the economy is bad, if you want to make 
the economy better, then be nice to nature and 
be nice to this beautiful blue sky, green plant world,

00:36:33.940 --> 00:36:36.540
because that will make your economy better.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:41.420
All right. See you at the next episode.  
Thank you, Tim. Thank you, Seiji.

